       These  routines  manipulate the current attributes of the named window,
       which then apply to all characters that are  written  into  the  window
       with  wwaaddddcchh,  wwaaddddssttrr  and  wwpprriinnttww.  Attributes are a property of the
       character, and move with the character through any  scrolling  and  in‐
       sert/delete  line/character  operations.   To the extent possible, they
       are displayed as appropriate modifications to the graphic rendition  of
       characters put on the screen.

       These  routines do not affect the attributes used when erasing portions
       of the window.  See ccuurrss__bbkkggdd(3X) for functions which  modify  the  at‐
       tributes used for erasing and clearing.

   WWiinnddooww AAttttrriibbuutteess
       There are two sets of functions:

       •   functions   for  manipulating  the  window  attributes  and  color:
           wwaattttrr__sseett and wwaattttrr__ggeett.

       •   functions for manipulating only the window attributes (not  color):
           wwaattttrr__oonn and wwaattttrr__ooffff.

       The  wwaattttrr__sseett function sets the current attributes of the given window
       to _a_t_t_r_s, with color specified by _p_a_i_r.

       Use wwaattttrr__ggeett to retrieve attributes for the given window.

       Use aattttrr__oonn and wwaattttrr__oonn to turn on  window  attributes,  i.e.,  values
       OR'd  together  in  _a_t_t_r,  without  affecting  other  attributes.   Use
       aattttrr__ooffff and wwaattttrr__ooffff to turn off window attributes, again values OR'd
       together in _a_t_t_r, without affecting other attributes.

   LLeeggaaccyy WWiinnddooww AAttttrriibbuutteess
       The  X/Open  window attribute routines which _s_e_t or _g_e_t, turn _o_n or _o_f_f
       are extensions of older routines which assume that color pairs are OR'd
       into  the attribute parameter.  These newer routines use similar names,
       because X/Open simply added an underscore (__) for the newer names.

       The iinntt datatype used in the legacy routines is treated as if it is the
       same size as cchhttyyppee (used by aaddddcchh(3X)).  It holds the common video at‐
       tributes (such as bold, reverse), as well as  a  few  bits  for  color.
       Those bits correspond to the AA__CCOOLLOORR symbol.  The CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRR macro pro‐
       vides a value which can be OR'd into the attribute parameter.  For  ex‐
       ample,  as  long  as  that value fits into the AA__CCOOLLOORR mask, then these
       calls produce similar results:

           attrset(A_BOLD | COLOR_PAIR(_p_a_i_r));
           attr_set(A_BOLD, _p_a_i_r, NULL);

       However, if the value does not fit, then the CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRR macro uses only
       the  bits  that fit.  For example, because in _n_c_u_r_s_e_s AA__CCOOLLOORR has eight
       (8) bits, then CCOOLLOORR__PPAAIIRR((_2_5_9)) is 4 (i.e., 259 is 4 more than the limit
       255).

       The  PPAAIIRR__NNUUMMBBEERR  macro extracts a pair number from an iinntt (or cchhttyyppee).
       For example, the _i_n_p_u_t and _o_u_t_p_u_t values in these statements  would  be
       the same:

           int value = A_BOLD | COLOR_PAIR(_i_n_p_u_t);
           int _o_u_t_p_u_t = PAIR_NUMBER(value);

       The  aattttrrsseett routine is a legacy feature predating SVr4 curses but kept
       in X/Open Curses for the same reason that SVr4 curses kept it: compati‐
       bility.

       The  remaining  aattttrr*  functions operate exactly like the corresponding
       aattttrr__* functions, except that they take arguments of  type  iinntt  rather
       than aattttrr__tt.

       There  is  no  corresponding aattttrrggeett function as such in X/Open Curses,
       although _n_c_u_r_s_e_s provides ggeettaattttrrss (see ccuurrss__lleeggaaccyy(3X)).

