       The  new  functions are intended to work with a new series of highlight
       macros prefixed with WWAA__.  The older macros have direct counterparts in
       the newer set of names:

              Lb  Lb  Lb  Lx.  Name Description _ WA_NORMAL Normal display (no
              highlight) WA_STANDOUT    T{ Best highlighting mode of the  ter‐
              minal T} WA_UNDERLINE   Underlining WA_REVERSE     Reverse video
              WA_BLINK  Blinking WA_DIM    Half bright WA_BOLD   Extra  bright
              or bold WA_ALTCHARSET  Alternate character set

       X/Open  Curses  does  not  assign  values to these symbols, nor does it
       state whether or not they are related to the similarly-named  A_NORMAL,
       etc.:

       •   X/Open  Curses  specifies  that  each  pair of corresponding AA__ and
           WWAA__-using functions operates on the same current-highlight informa‐
           tion.

       •   However, in some implementations, those symbols have unrelated val‐
           ues.

           For example, the Solaris _x_p_g_4 (X/Open) curses declares aattttrr__tt to be
           an unsigned short integer (16-bits), while cchhttyyppee is a unsigned in‐
           teger (32-bits).  The WWAA__ symbols in this case are  different  from
           the  AA__  symbols because they are used for a smaller datatype which
           does not represent AA__CCHHAARRTTEEXXTT or AA__CCOOLLOORR.

           In this implementation (as in many others), the values happen to be
           the  same  because it simplifies copying information between cchhttyyppee
           and cccchhaarr__tt variables.

       •   Because _n_c_u_r_s_e_s's aattttrr__tt can hold a  color  pair  (in  the  AA__CCOOLLOORR
           field),  a  call to wwaattttrr__oonn, wwaattttrr__ooffff, or wwaattttrr__sseett may alter the
           window's color.  If the color pair information in the attribute pa‐
           rameter is zero, no change is made to the window's color.

           This is consistent with SVr4 curses; X/Open Curses does not specify
           this.

       The X/Open Curses extended conformance level adds new highlights AA__HHOORR‐‐
       IIZZOONNTTAALL,  AA__LLEEFFTT,  AA__LLOOWW, AA__RRIIGGHHTT, AA__TTOOPP, AA__VVEERRTTIICCAALL (and corresponding
       WWAA__ macros for each).  As of August 2013, no  known  terminal  provides
       these highlights (i.e., via the ssggrr11 capability).

HHIISSTTOORRYY
       X/Open  Curses  is  largely  based  on  SVr4 curses, adding support for
       “wide-characters” (not specific to Unicode).  Some of the  X/Open  dif‐
       ferences  from  SVr4 curses address the way video attributes can be ap‐
       plied to wide-characters.  But aside from that,  aattttrrsseett  and  aattttrr__sseett
       are  similar.  SVr4 curses provided the basic features for manipulating
       video attributes.  However, earlier versions of curses provided a  part
       of these features.

       As  seen  in  2.8BSD, curses assumed 7-bit characters, using the eighth
       bit of a byte to represent the _s_t_a_n_d_o_u_t feature (often  implemented  as
       bold  and/or reverse video).  The BSD curses library provided functions
       ssttaannddoouutt and ssttaannddeenndd which were carried along into X/Open  Curses  due
       to their pervasive use in legacy applications.

       Some  terminals  in  the  1980s  could  support  a variety of video at‐
       tributes, although the BSD curses library could do nothing with  those.
       System V (1983) provided an improved curses library.  It defined the AA__
       symbols for use by applications to  manipulate  the  other  attributes.
       There are few useful references for the chronology.

       Goodheart's  book  _U_N_I_X  _C_u_r_s_e_s _E_x_p_l_a_i_n_e_d (1991) describes SVr3 (1987),
       commenting on several functions:

       •   the aattttrroonn, aattttrrooffff, aattttrrsseett functions (and most of  the  functions
           found in SVr4 but not in BSD curses) were introduced by System V,

       •   the  alternate character set feature with AA__AALLTTCCHHAARRSSEETT was added in
           SVr2 and improved in SVr3 (by adding aaccss__mmaapp[[]]),

       •   ssttaarrtt__ccoolloorr and related color-functions were introduced  by  System
           V.3.2,

       •   pads, soft-keys were added in SVr3, and

       Goodheart did not mention the background character or the cccchhaarr__tt type.
       Those are respectively SVr4 and X/Open features.  He did mention the AA__
       constants,  but did not indicate their values.  Those were not the same
       in different systems, even for those marked as System V.

